1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:08,788
If you wear wireless headphones, their signal is jumping from one channel to another non-stop. Sometimes, over a thousand times per second.

2
00:00:09,008 --> 00:00:15,036
That idea was patented in the middle of a war by one of Hollywood's most famous actresses. Alongside a pianist.

3
00:00:15,486 --> 00:00:18,478
And the United States Navy locked it away in a drawer.

4
00:00:18,928 --> 00:00:21,830
They suggested she would help more with her face.

5
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:30,003
This is the story of Hedy Lamarr. The most beautiful woman in the world, according to her movie studio. And an inventor.

6
00:00:30,853 --> 00:00:33,845
Chapter One. The Music Box.

7
00:00:34,195 --> 00:00:41,251
She was born in Vienna in 1914 as Hedwig Kiesler. The only child of a bank director and a pianist.

8
00:00:41,471 --> 00:00:49,106
Her father took her on walks and explained how things worked: streetcars, printing presses, electricity.

9
00:00:49,326 --> 00:00:55,801
Her mother taught her the piano. Those eighty-eight keys would serve her far more than anyone imagined.

10
00:00:56,021 --> 00:01:01,826
At age five, she took apart her music box to understand how it made sound. And put it back together.

11
00:01:02,046 --> 00:01:09,950
As a teenager she chose acting. And at eighteen, she starred in a film so scandalous it was banned in several countries.

12
00:01:10,850 --> 00:01:15,360
All of Europe talked about her face. Nobody talked about her mind.

13
00:01:16,210 --> 00:01:19,068
Chapter Two. The Golden Cage.

14
00:01:19,418 --> 00:01:26,964
Among those who saw her act was Fritz Mandl, one of Austria's largest arms manufacturers. They married that same year.

15
00:01:27,184 --> 00:01:32,543
Mandl was jealous and controlling. She lived surrounded by luxury... and under constant watch.

16
00:01:32,763 --> 00:01:41,113
They say he tried to buy up every copy of that scandalous film so no one else could see it. He failed: they kept making new prints.

17
00:01:41,333 --> 00:01:48,031
In that house, there were dinners with military officers and arms buyers. Among his clients was Mussolini's government.

18
00:01:48,251 --> 00:01:55,485
She was treated like part of the decor. But she listened. They talked about radio-guided weapons and how to jam them.

19
00:01:55,705 --> 00:02:01,019
In 1937 she escaped. According to her, disguised as a maid.

20
00:02:01,919 --> 00:02:06,429
She left with the clothes on her back. And with everything she had heard at those dinners.

21
00:02:07,279 --> 00:02:10,583
Chapter Three. The Most Beautiful Woman in the World.

22
00:02:10,933 --> 00:02:19,149
In London she got a meeting with Louis B. Mayer, head of the MGM studio. He offered her a small contract. She said no.

23
00:02:19,369 --> 00:02:23,522
Then she booked passage on the very same ship taking Mayer back to New York.

24
00:02:23,742 --> 00:02:30,083
By the time they disembarked, she had a contract four times better and a new name: Hedy Lamarr.

25
00:02:30,303 --> 00:02:38,966
Her first American movie was an instant hit. The studio marketed her as the most beautiful woman in the world, making several films a year.

26
00:02:39,186 --> 00:02:47,759
But on set she got bored. Between takes, she invented: a tablet that turned water into soda, an improved traffic light.

27
00:02:47,979 --> 00:02:56,240
Millionaire Howard Hughes gave her equipment for her dressing room. She sketched him a faster wing, combining the fastest fish and the fastest bird.

28
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:59,586
According to her, Hughes told her she was a genius.

29
00:03:00,486 --> 00:03:05,800
Hollywood paid her to sit still. She didn't know how to sit still.

30
00:03:06,650 --> 00:03:09,910
Chapter Four. The Torpedo Problem.

31
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:17,226
In 1940, a German submarine sank a ship carrying British children to Canada. Most of them died.

32
00:03:17,446 --> 00:03:21,554
Hedy wanted to help. And she remembered those dinners in Vienna.

33
00:03:22,004 --> 00:03:28,300
A radio-guided torpedo is like a remote-controlled car underwater. The ship sends it commands.

34
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:37,093
Those commands travel on a radio wave. Its frequency is how many times it vibrates per second, and each station vibrates at its own rhythm.

35
00:03:37,313 --> 00:03:42,493
That is why we call each frequency a channel. And the torpedo listens to just one.

36
00:03:42,713 --> 00:03:51,421
The problem: the enemy can find that channel and flood it with noise. Like someone shouting over your voice. The torpedo goes deaf.

37
00:03:52,321 --> 00:03:55,759
What was needed was a message no one could find.

38
00:03:56,609 --> 00:04:00,181
Chapter Five. A Piano Inside a Torpedo.

39
00:04:00,531 --> 00:04:06,604
At a dinner party she met George Antheil, a composer who called himself the bad boy of music.

40
00:04:06,824 --> 00:04:13,746
Years earlier he had written a piece for sixteen player pianos performing in sync, with sirens and airplane propellers.

41
00:04:13,966 --> 00:04:21,423
Antheil also wanted to help. His brother had died in the opening months of the war, when the plane he was aboard was shot down.

42
00:04:21,643 --> 00:04:27,850
She had the core idea: don't let the signal stay still. Make it hop from channel to channel all the time.

43
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:33,747
Imagine two friends changing radio stations at the exact same moment, following a secret list.

44
00:04:33,967 --> 00:04:41,916
A spy listening to one station hears only a tiny blip of noise, and nothing more. To jam them, you would have to block every channel at once.

45
00:04:42,136 --> 00:04:50,665
But how do you synchronize the ship and the torpedo? Antheil proposed perforated paper rolls, like in player pianos: one on each side.

46
00:04:51,165 --> 00:04:55,050
Eighty-eight frequencies. One for each key of the piano.

47
00:04:55,270 --> 00:05:09,469
They worked for months on her dining room table, with help from an electrical engineering professor. In 1942 they received the patent. She signed with her married name at the time: Hedy Kiesler Markey.

48
00:05:10,369 --> 00:05:14,879
Because of that, for decades, almost no one knew the idea was hers.

49
00:05:15,729 --> 00:05:18,007
Chapter Six. Bonds and Kisses.

50
00:05:18,357 --> 00:05:25,814
They donated the patent to the Navy to help the war effort. The Navy rejected it, claiming the mechanism wouldn't fit inside a torpedo.

51
00:05:26,034 --> 00:05:30,276
Antheil suspected the mistake was mentioning a piano in front of naval officers.

52
00:05:30,496 --> 00:05:35,363
They suggested Hedy help in another way: selling war bonds with her fame.

53
00:05:35,583 --> 00:05:42,773
She did. She kissed anyone who bought twenty-five thousand dollars in bonds. In a single night she raised seven million.

54
00:05:42,993 --> 00:05:47,056
On a tour across several cities, she was credited with raising around twenty-five million.

55
00:05:47,276 --> 00:05:53,081
One detail: the sailor chosen at random for the kiss was always the same guy. He was part of the show.

56
00:05:53,981 --> 00:05:58,134
Her face raised millions. Her idea stayed in a drawer.

57
00:05:58,984 --> 00:06:01,440
Chapter Seven. The Drawer.

58
00:06:01,790 --> 00:06:07,684
The patent expired in 1959. That same year, Antheil passed away.

59
00:06:07,904 --> 00:06:14,066
By then, engineers had rescued the concept and made it fully electronic, without paper rolls.

60
00:06:14,286 --> 00:06:22,681
In 1962 came the Cuban Missile Crisis. United States warships were already using a version of that system.

61
00:06:22,901 --> 00:06:31,921
Hedy was paid nothing: the patent had expired and almost no one knew it was hers. Her acting career faded, and she lived her final years in deep isolation.

62
00:06:32,141 --> 00:06:41,027
Meanwhile, her idea kept growing. In the eighties and nineties, frequency hopping became a cornerstone of wireless communications.

63
00:06:41,927 --> 00:06:46,080
The world filled up with her idea. And forgot her name.

64
00:06:46,930 --> 00:06:50,190
Chapter Eight. Sixteen Hundred Hops.

65
00:06:50,540 --> 00:07:01,525
In 1997, at age eighty-two, she received a pioneer award of the digital age alongside Antheil. She no longer appeared in public, so she didn't attend.

66
00:07:01,745 --> 00:07:12,908
She died in the year 2000. In 2014 she was inducted into the National Inventors Hall of Fame. And the following year, Google dedicated its homepage to her on her birthday.

67
00:07:13,358 --> 00:07:21,798
Today, classic Bluetooth spreads its signal across seventy-nine channels. And hops between them up to sixteen hundred times per second.

68
00:07:22,018 --> 00:07:30,993
That's why your headphones barely drop out even near Wi-Fi or a microwave: if one channel is busy, they jump to another.

69
00:07:31,213 --> 00:07:42,957
The paper roll is now a microchip. Her idea belongs to a family of techniques used by Bluetooth and countless radios. The trick is the same: jumping together, in secret.

70
00:07:43,607 --> 00:07:48,430
So the next time you connect your headphones, think of a piano inside a torpedo.

71
00:07:49,330 --> 00:07:52,232
And of a woman who was told to sit still.

72
00:07:53,182 --> 00:07:58,943
And now we're reading you in the comments: what story would you like to see in the next episode?
